{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"A\":\"Research end-to-end input and storage costs without repeating the established O(N²) window algorithm: compare the current contiguous-matrix approach with a correct fast integer reader and, if useful, a streaming formulation. Preserve signed input handling and 64-bit accumulation. The measured ring-buffer candidate was slower than the flat-matrix candidate, so treat reduced memory as a hypothesis, not a gain; measure the complete program across the supplied cases.\",\"B\":\"Refine the strongest measured candidate, 5d7be42b7d394a6a (55.25 ms): retain its rolling column-band method and investigate the hot horizontal scan, band updates, and small-S fallback. Measure changes independently and end-to-end. The prefix-based horizontal scan candidate was slower (57.07 ms), so do not assume SIMD reductions or altered bounds help; preserve correct handling of negative values and all valid S.\",\"C\":\"Develop the independent 64-bit prefix-sum approach represented by a825e102b5b64273 (55.58 ms). Explore cache-aware tiling and work partitioning appropriate to the four-core ARM64 target, while measuring memory traffic, parallel overhead, and full-program time. Keep all window sums exact for every valid input; compare against the rolling-band leader rather than assuming prefix sums or added parallelism are faster.\",\"G\":\"Use 5d7be42b7d394a6a as the current measured reference, not as a guaranteed final winner. Integrate only independently tested, complementary changes from A/B/C; rerun correctness and full end-to-end measurements on the integrated program because interactions may erase gains. If no integration beats the best standalone candidate reliably, retain that candidate. Do not specialize to test inputs or change the harness, target, compiler, or budgets.\",\"analysis\":\"The source’s serpentine scan performs O(SN²) work. Measured O(N²) candidates reduce mean time from 125.39 ms baseline to 55.25–55.91 ms; the best is B’s rolling column-band variant, narrowly ahead of C’s prefix-sum variant at 55.58 ms. Other measured variants are slower (57.07–57.41 ms), showing that similar algorithms and added SIMD/parallel work do not guarantee gains. The target is four-core ARM64 with no GPU, so prioritize whole-program measurements and exact general correctness over assumed additive speedups.\"}"}}
